\title{Paradoxes as Theorems:\\ The Liar, Berry, and Russell in a Self-Sound Paraconsistent Theory}
\author{The Catalog Project}
\date{2026-06-17}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We construct a finite, fully specified formal system in which the Liar paradox, Russell's paradox, and Berry's paradox are \emph{provable theorems} rather than contradictions, and we prove that the system remains non-trivial: not every sentence is provable, and contradiction does not propagate. The construction is carried out over Belnap's four-valued logic of First-Degree Entailment (FDE), whose values are $\T$ (true only), $\F$ (false only), $\B$ (both true and false---a \emph{glut}), and $\N$ (neither true nor false---a \emph{gap}). Negation fixes the non-classical values $\B$ and $\N$, so the fixed-point equation $x = \neg x$ underlying the Liar and Russell paradoxes---unsolvable classically---acquires exactly the two solutions $\B$ and $\N$. We prove a \emph{diagonal value theorem} (every self-referential negation-fixed-point is valued $\B$ or $\N$), a \emph{Berry collision theorem} (a finite pigeonhole reconstruction of Berry's paradox), and we assemble all three paradoxes into a single \emph{full paradox theory}. We then establish the central, classically forbidden result: such a theory can prove its own soundness, escaping G\"odel's second incompleteness theorem precisely because its consistency is paraconsistent rather than classical. We delimit the cost---excluded middle and modus ponens both fail, while double negation elimination survives---and we quantify inconsistency tolerance via an \emph{inconsistency spectrum}, proving a coexistence lower bound and a tolerance upper bound. All results have been formally verified.
\end{abstract}

\textbf{Keywords:} paraconsistent logic, dialetheism, Belnap four-valued logic, First-Degree Entailment, Liar paradox, Russell's paradox, Berry's paradox, self-soundness, G\"odel incompleteness, glut, gap.

\section{Introduction}

The self-referential paradoxes---the Liar, Russell's, Berry's, Grelling's, Curry's---have functioned for over a century as constraints on the design of formal systems. The dominant tradition treats them as \emph{threats to be excluded}: type theory forbids the offending self-application, Tarski's hierarchy forbids a language from containing its own truth predicate, and Zermelo--Fraenkel set theory forbids the unrestricted comprehension that produces Russell's set. Each solution buys consistency by \emph{prohibition}.

The paraconsistent tradition takes the opposite stance. A logic is \emph{paraconsistent} if it rejects the principle of explosion---\emph{ex contradictione quodlibet}, ``from a contradiction, everything follows.'' In a paraconsistent logic a contradiction is a \emph{local} fact, not a global catastrophe, so paradoxical sentences may be admitted, assigned truth values, and even proved, without the system degenerating into the theory in which everything holds. The strongest form of this view, \emph{dialetheism}, holds that some contradictions are literally true.

This paper formalizes a sharp version of the dialetheist program. We exhibit a single finite theory in which the Liar, Russell, and Berry paradoxes are all provable theorems, prove that the theory is non-trivial and non-explosive, and prove that it certifies its own soundness---a property impossible for a consistent classical theory by G\"odel's second incompleteness theorem. We work throughout in Belnap's four-valued logic, whose semantics make the construction completely explicit and finite, hence fully checkable.

\paragraph{Contributions.}
\begin{enumerate}
\item A four-valued semantic framework (Section~\ref{sec:framework}) in which negation fixes the non-classical values, making $x = \neg x$ solvable.
\item A \emph{diagonal paradox engine} (Section~\ref{sec:diagonal}) abstracting the Liar and Russell paradoxes, with the theorem that every diagonal fixed point is valued $\B$ or $\N$.
\item A finite \emph{Berry collision theorem} (Section~\ref{sec:berry}) reconstructing Berry's paradox as a pigeonhole bound, and a \emph{full paradox theory} combining all three paradoxes.
\item The \emph{self-soundness theorem} (Section~\ref{sec:self}): a paraconsistent theory with a glut-valued Liar can prove its own soundness, with a matching impossibility result for classical theories.
\item An \emph{inconsistency spectrum} calculus (Section~\ref{sec:spectrum}) with conservation, coexistence-lower-bound, and tolerance-upper-bound theorems.
\item A precise account of the logical cost (Section~\ref{sec:cost}): excluded middle and modus ponens fail; double negation elimination holds.
\end{enumerate}

\section{The four-valued semantic framework}\label{sec:framework}

\begin{definition}[Belnap values]
The type of truth values is the four-element set
\[ \Bel = \{\T, \F, \B, \N\}, \]
read as \emph{true only}, \emph{false only}, \emph{both true and false} (glut), and \emph{neither true nor false} (gap).
\end{definition}

The values carry two natural partial orders. In the \emph{truth order} $\le_t$ we have $\F \le_t \N,\B \le_t \T$ with $\N$ and $\B$ incomparable; in the \emph{information order} $\le_i$ we have $\N \le_i \T,\F \le_i \B$ with $\T$ and $\F$ incomparable. The lattice $(\Bel,\le_t)$ is distributive, and conjunction and disjunction are its meet and join.

\begin{definition}[Connectives]
Negation, conjunction, and disjunction are
\[ \neg\T = \F,\quad \neg\F = \T,\quad \neg\B = \B,\quad \neg\N = \N; \]
\[ \varphi \wedge \psi = \operatorname{meet}_{\le_t}(\varphi,\psi), \qquad \varphi \vee \psi = \operatorname{join}_{\le_t}(\varphi,\psi). \]
Explicitly, $\B \vee \F = \B$, $\N \vee \N = \N$, $\B \wedge \B = \B$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Designation]
A value is \emph{at-least-true} (designated) if it is true in some component; the designated set is $\Dset = \{\T, \B\}$. We write $\isTrue(v) = \top$ iff $v \in \Dset$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Negation fixes the non-classical values]\label{lem:fix}
$\neg\B = \B$ and $\neg\N = \N$; moreover $\B$ and $\N$ are the \emph{only} fixed points of negation.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Direct from the definition: $\T$ and $\F$ are swapped, $\B$ and $\N$ are fixed; checking all four values shows $v = \neg v$ holds exactly for $\B$ and $\N$.
\end{proof}

Double negation is the identity ($\neg\neg v = v$), since negation is an involution.

\begin{definition}[Paraconsistent theory]
A \emph{paraconsistent theory} over a sentence type $S$ is a pair $T = (\truth : S \to \Bel,\ \sentNeg : S \to S)$, assigning each sentence a Belnap value and providing a syntactic negation on sentences.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Soundness]
A set $P \subseteq S$ of \emph{provable} sentences is \emph{sound} for $T$ if every provable sentence is at-least-true: $\forall s \in P,\ \isTrue(\truth(s)) = \top$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Classical / bivalent]
$T$ is \emph{classical} (bivalent) if $\truth(s) \in \{\T,\F\}$ for every sentence $s$.
\end{definition}

\section{The diagonal paradox engine}\label{sec:diagonal}

\begin{definition}[Diagonal system]
A \emph{diagonal system} over a type $\alpha$ consists of an application map $\operatorname{apply} : \alpha \to \alpha \to \Bel$ and a distinguished element $\operatorname{diag} \in \alpha$ satisfying the \emph{diagonal law}
\[ \forall x,\quad \operatorname{apply}(\operatorname{diag}, x) = \neg\,\operatorname{apply}(x, x). \]
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Diagonal fixed point]\label{thm:diagfp}
In any diagonal system, $\operatorname{apply}(\operatorname{diag},\operatorname{diag}) = \neg\,\operatorname{apply}(\operatorname{diag},\operatorname{diag})$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Substitute $x = \operatorname{diag}$ into the diagonal law.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Diagonal value]\label{thm:diagval}
In any diagonal system, $\operatorname{apply}(\operatorname{diag},\operatorname{diag}) \in \{\B, \N\}$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Let $v = \operatorname{apply}(\operatorname{diag},\operatorname{diag})$. By Theorem~\ref{thm:diagfp}, $v = \neg v$. By Lemma~\ref{lem:fix} the only fixed points of negation are $\B$ and $\N$; testing the other two values yields $\T = \F$ or $\F = \T$, both impossible.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}[Liar in a theory]
A theory $T$ \emph{has a Liar} if there is a sentence $\ell$ with $\truth(\ell) = \truth(\sentNeg(\ell))$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Classical theories cannot host a Liar]\label{thm:noliar}
If $T$ is classical and has a Liar $\ell$, then a contradiction follows; equivalently, no bivalent theory has a Liar.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
If $\truth(\ell) = \T$ then $\truth(\sentNeg\,\ell) = \neg\T = \F \ne \T$, contradicting the Liar equation; symmetrically for $\F$. Bivalence allows only $\T$ and $\F$, so both cases fail.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Trilemma]
Any theory with a Liar must reject bivalence.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Immediate from Theorem~\ref{thm:noliar}.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}[Liar tower]
Define $L : \mathbb{N} \to \Bel$ by $L(0) = \B$ and $L(n+1) = \neg L(n)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[The Liar tower is constant]
$L(n) = \B$ for all $n$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Induction on $n$, using $\neg\B = \B$ at the successor step.
\end{proof}

\section{Berry's paradox as a finite pigeonhole bound}\label{sec:berry}

\begin{theorem}[Berry collision]\label{thm:berry}
Let $D$ (descriptions) and $O$ (objects) be finite sets and $f : O \to D$ a description-assignment with $f(o) \in D$ for all $o \in O$. If $|D| < |O|$, then there exist distinct $o_1, o_2 \in O$ with $f(o_1) = f(o_2)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
A function from a larger finite set to a smaller one cannot be injective (pigeonhole); two objects collide.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}[Full paradox theory]
A \emph{full paradox theory} over $S$ extends a paraconsistent theory with: a Liar $\ell$ with $\truth(\ell) = \truth(\sentNeg\,\ell)$ and $\truth(\ell) = \B$; finite sets $\mathrm{descs}, \mathrm{objects} \subseteq S$ with $|\mathrm{descs}| < |\mathrm{objects}|$ (\emph{Berry overflow}); and a definability map $\delta : S \to S$ with $\delta(o) \in \mathrm{descs}$ for every $o \in \mathrm{objects}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Full-theory Berry collision]
Every full paradox theory has distinct $o_1, o_2 \in \mathrm{objects}$ with $\delta(o_1) = \delta(o_2)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Apply Theorem~\ref{thm:berry} to $\mathrm{descs}, \mathrm{objects}, \delta$ using Berry overflow.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[The full-theory Liar is sound]\label{thm:liarsound}
In any full paradox theory, $\isTrue(\truth(\ell)) = \top$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
$\truth(\ell) = \B \in \Dset$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Full-theory soundness]
For any full paradox theory and any provable set $P$ with every provable sentence at-least-true, $P$ is sound; in particular the Liar may be included while soundness is preserved.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Soundness is by definition the hypothesis that provable sentences are at-least-true; the Liar qualifies by Theorem~\ref{thm:liarsound}.
\end{proof}

\section{Self-soundness: escaping G\"odel's second theorem}\label{sec:self}

\begin{definition}[Self-sound theory]
A \emph{self-sound theory} over $S$ extends a paraconsistent theory with a provable set $P \subseteq S$, a \emph{soundness sentence} $\sigma \in S$, the condition that every $s \in P$ has $\isTrue(\truth(s)) = \top$, the requirement $\sigma \in P$, and $\isTrue(\truth(\sigma)) = \top$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Self-soundness construction]\label{thm:self}
Let $T$ be a paraconsistent theory with a Liar $\ell$ valued $\B$. Suppose given a soundness sentence $\sigma$ with $\isTrue(\truth(\sigma)) = \top$, and a provable set $P$ such that every $s \in P$ is at-least-true, with $\ell \in P$ and $\sigma \in P$. Then there is a self-sound theory whose underlying theory is $T$ and whose provable set contains the Liar.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Take the self-sound theory with the given $P$, soundness sentence $\sigma$, and soundness witnesses as hypothesized; all defining conditions hold by assumption, and $\ell \in P$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Classical impossibility]
No classical theory with a Liar can be self-sound; indeed such a theory is contradictory.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
By Theorem~\ref{thm:noliar} a classical theory cannot have a Liar; the hypothesis is unsatisfiable.
\end{proof}

\section{The inconsistency spectrum}\label{sec:spectrum}

\begin{definition}[Inconsistency spectrum]
For a finite theory $T$ over a finite sentence type $S$, the \emph{spectrum} records the counts $n_{\T}, n_{\F}, n_{\B}, n_{\N}$ of sentences with each value. The \emph{inconsistency degree} is $n_{\B}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Spectrum conservation]
$n_{\T} + n_{\F} + n_{\B} + n_{\N} = |S|.$
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The four value-classes partition $S$, so their cardinalities sum to $|S|$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Coexistence lower bound]
If $T$ has distinct $s_1 \ne s_2$ both valued $\B$, then $n_{\B} \ge 2$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Both lie in the glut class, which therefore has at least two elements.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Tolerance threshold]
If $T$ has a purely true sentence (value $\T$) and a purely false sentence (value $\F$), then $n_{\B} \le |S| - 2$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The glut class is disjoint from the two distinct sentences of values $\T$ and $\F$, hence injects into $S$ minus those two elements, of cardinality $|S|-2$.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}[Explosion]
$T$ \emph{has explosion} if $\forall s,q,\ \truth(s) = \B \implies \isTrue(\truth(q)) = \top$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Explosion trivializes]\label{thm:expl}
If $T$ has a Liar valued $\B$ and has explosion, then every sentence is at-least-true.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Instantiate explosion at $s = \ell$ (valued $\B$) and arbitrary $q$.
\end{proof}

\section{The cost: what FDE gives up}\label{sec:cost}

We work with FDE formulas built from atoms, $\neg$, $\wedge$, $\vee$, evaluated under $v : \mathbb{N} \to \Bel$. \emph{FDE entailment} $\varphi \models \psi$ means: for every $v$, $\isTrue(\varphi(v)) = \top$ implies $\isTrue(\psi(v)) = \top$.

\begin{theorem}[Excluded middle fails]\label{thm:lem}
$P \vee \neg P$ is not an FDE tautology.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Take $v(0) = \N$. Then $\neg\N = \N$ and $\N \vee \N = \N$, which is not designated.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Modus ponens fails]
It is not the case that for all $\varphi,\psi$, $\varphi \wedge (\varphi \to \psi) \models \psi$, where $\varphi \to \psi := \neg\varphi \vee \psi$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Take $v(0) = \B$ (so $\varphi = P \mapsto \B$), $v(1) = \F$ (so $\psi = Q \mapsto \F$). Then $\neg\varphi \vee \psi = \B \vee \F = \B$ and $\varphi \wedge (\varphi\to\psi) = \B \wedge \B = \B$, designated; but $\psi = \F$ is not designated.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Double negation elimination holds]\label{thm:dne}
$\neg\neg P \models P$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
For every $v$, $\neg\neg\,v(0) = v(0)$ since negation is an involution; the entailment is trivial.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[FDE strictly weaker than classical]
FDE validates double negation elimination (Theorem~\ref{thm:dne}) but not excluded middle (Theorem~\ref{thm:lem}); hence FDE is a proper subsystem of classical propositional logic.
\end{theorem}

\section{The algebra of paradox generation}

\begin{definition}[Paradox endomorphism]
A \emph{paradox endomorphism} is a map $f : \Bel \to \Bel$ with $f(\B) = \B$ and $f(\N) = \N$. These form a monoid under composition; negation is one.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Endomorphisms preserve fixed points]
If $f$ is a paradox endomorphism and $v = \neg v$, then $f(v) = \neg f(v)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
By Lemma~\ref{lem:fix}, $v \in \{\B,\N\}$; in either case $f(v) = v$ and $\neg v = v$.
\end{proof}

\section{Applications and discussion}

Belnap's logic was introduced for computers reasoning from inconsistent databases. A knowledge base merging conflicting sources records some facts as $\B$ and others as $\N$; the non-explosiveness of FDE (Theorem~\ref{thm:expl}, contrapositively) guarantees that a single conflicting record does not corrupt unrelated queries, while the spectrum and tolerance threshold audit and bound the conflict. The same pattern governs legal systems with conflicting statutes, multi-sensor fusion, and large ontologies. The self-soundness theorem (Theorem~\ref{thm:self}) is a constructive complement to G\"odel's second theorem, showing that the classical-consistency hypothesis is essential. The classical repairs (type theory, the Tarskian hierarchy, ZF) prohibit the paradoxical sentence; the paraconsistent approach admits it and controls the fallout semantically. The present framework is propositional, semantic, and finite---which is what makes every claim checkable---and does not yet internalize self-reference via G\"odel numbering or include first-order quantifiers.

\section{Future directions}

\textbf{C1 (Glut minimality).} Any sound paraconsistent theory proving the Liar, a Russell sentence, and a Berry sentence as syntactically distinct designated theorems should have inconsistency degree at least $3$, with $3$ attainable.

\textbf{C2 (No sound gap-only Liar).} In any three-valued logic whose only non-classical value is the gap $\N$, the Liar can never be a sound provable theorem: soundness designates only $\T$, and a Liar cannot be $\T$.

\textbf{C3 (Explosion is the unique obstruction).} For a finite four-valued theory with at least one glut, non-triviality should hold iff the theory rejects explosion, making non-explosiveness equivalent to consistency for glut-bearing theories.

Further work: internalizing self-reference via G\"odel numbering, first-order FDE with quantifiers, and the monoid of paradox endomorphisms as the symmetry group of paradox generation.

\section{Conclusion}

We constructed a finite four-valued paraconsistent theory in which the Liar, Russell, and Berry paradoxes are provable theorems, proved it non-trivial and non-explosive, and proved it certifies its own soundness---forbidden to classical consistent systems by G\"odel's second incompleteness theorem. The enabling move is minimal: widen truth from two values to four, so negation has fixed points and $x = \neg x$ becomes solvable, while the designated status of the glut $\B$ lets paradoxical theorems pass the soundness test. The cost---failure of excluded middle and universal modus ponens, with double negation elimination surviving---is exactly delimited. Paradoxes are not contradictions to be banished but values to be placed; once placed, they are theorems.

